    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently working on a site in the Peloponnese and I'm looking at a collection of highly fragmented ceramic shards from a single stratum. I'm having some trouble distinguishing between different firing techniques used in this period. Could you explain the key indicators to look for when trying to differentiate between oxidized and reduced firing atmospheres in terracotta fragments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To distinguish between oxidized and reduced firing atmospheres, look for the color and surface texture of the shards; oxidized firing typically turns terracotta reddish-orange due to the presence of iron oxides reacting with abundant oxygen. In contrast, reduced firing often results in darker, purplish, or nearly black colors as carbon monoxide limits oxygen, but this can vary significantly based on the specific clay body and firing trajectory. You should also examine the surface sheen, as reduced fire sometimes creates harder, glossier surfaces compared to the more porous texture of standard oxidized products. Since your shards are highly fragmented, checking for consistent color variations across the same stratum can help identify if the kiln atmosphere was uniform or if multiple firing events occurred. Consulting local examples or digital resources specific to Peloponnesian pottery from that exact layer will be the most reliable way to confirm your observations.",
